Using kinetic energy conservation:
\[
\frac{1}{2} m v^2 = e \Delta \phi = 100 \text{ keV} = 100 \times 1.602 \times 10^{-16} \text{ J}
\]
\[
v^2 = \frac{2 e \Delta \phi}{m}
\]
For an electron: \( m = 9.109 \times 10^{-31} \text{ kg} \), \( e = 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C} \)
\[
v = \sqrt{\frac{2 (1.602 \times 10^{-19})(100 \times 10^3)}{9.109 \times 10^{-31}}}
= \sqrt{\frac{3.204 \times 10^{-14}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31}}}
= \sqrt{3.516 \times 10^{16}}
= 1.875 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}
\]
This is approximately \(1.88 \times 10^8 \text{ m/s}\).

Answer: B
